            <div xml:id="d129">

                <pb facs="0216" n="950" sameAs="frus:frus1895p2/pg950"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Baron Fava to Mr.
                            Olney.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Personal.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Embassy of Italy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1895-10-01"><hi rendition="#italic">October, 1895</hi></date>.
                        (Received October 18.)</dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Olney</hi>:</p>
                <p>With reference to our last conversation, I have the honor to transmit herewith to
                    your excellency, accompanied by a summary, the statements which have been
                    forwarded to me by my Government relative to the family situation of the five
                    Italians lynched at Walsenburg, Colo., in March last.</p>
                <p>It results from the said statements, delivered by the municipal authorities of
                    the birthplaces of these individuals, that two of the three Italians who have
                    been killed were not married, but have left numerous relatives, and one was
                    married and has left a wife with three children. Of the other two Italians who
                    had their frozen feet amputated at the hospital, one has a son, his wife being
                    dead, and the other has a wife and a daughter.</p>
                <p>In submitting these official documents to your excellency’s examination, I leave
                    to your high and benevolent appreciation to suggest the amount which may be
                    deemed suitable to indemnify the families of the victims of the Colorado mob,
                    according to the spirit of justice which prompts all your actions.</p>
                <p>It will give me much pleasure to know at your earliest convenience your
                    excellency’s views on this subject.</p>
                <p>With my best thanks in advance, believe me, etc.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Fava.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>






